"""Explain saved full-design diagnostics and matched-cohort sensitivity fits."""
import csv
import hashlib
import json
from pathlib import Path
import shutil
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
BASE=ROOT/'eval/outputs/releases/2026-09-29-graded-reporting'
DIAGNOSTICS=BASE/'diagnostics.json'
EFFECTS=BASE/'effects.csv'
OUT=ROOT/'ufscthesisx/ufscthesisx/chapters/collinearity_explanation.tex'
RELEASE=ROOT/'eval/outputs/releases/2026-10-11-collinearity-explanation-v2'
diags={r['fit']:r for r in json.loads(DIAGNOSTICS.read_text()) if r['outcome']=='score'}
effects={(r['fit'],r['predictor']):r for r in csv.DictReader(EFFECTS.open())}
pooled=diags['pooled'];single=diags['pooled_single_spec_score']
assert pooled['n']==single['n']==diags['pooled_single_distance']['n']
for key,predictor in [('pooled_single_distance','distance'),('pooled_single_spec_score','spec_score')]:
 assert next(r for r in pooled['scaling'] if r['predictor']==predictor)==next(r for r in diags[key]['scaling'] if r['predictor']==predictor)
vd,vs=pooled['substantive_vif']['distance'],pooled['substantive_vif']['spec_score']
v_single=single['substantive_vif']['spec_score']
ifv=diags['components']['substantive_vif']['if_score']
consv=diags['components']['substantive_vif']['cons_score']
num=lambda fit,p:f"{float(effects[fit,p]['estimate']):.3f}"
text=r'''The practical question is whether the regression can separate the distance association from the specialization association. It includes distance, SPEC, model identity, and attack language. Some of these variables may contain overlapping information. For example, if SPEC is consistently higher for certain models, differences associated with SPEC also coincide with differences between those models. The model then has less information with which to distinguish the SPEC association from their baseline differences. This overlap is called \emph{collinearity}.

We check that overlap using the \emph{variance inflation factor} (VIF). A value of one indicates no linear overlap between a predictor and the other predictors; larger values indicate greater overlap.\footnote{VIF is calculated as $1/(1-R^2)$ by regressing the predictor on the other fitted design columns. This auxiliary $R^2$ concerns the predictor, not StrongREJECT scores. VIF is a design diagnostic; it does not directly multiply the robust GEE standard errors.} Here, ``other predictors'' includes the model and language indicators, as well as the other substantive predictor.

'''
text+=(f"Table~\\ref{{tab:graded-diagnostics}} reports distance VIF {vd:.2f} and SPEC VIF {vs:.2f} in the pooled fit. Distance has comparatively less overlap; SPEC has substantial overlap, making its separate adjusted contribution harder to isolate. This does not imply that SPEC is unimportant. Because its VIF exceeds the chosen threshold of five, we check simpler fits that include only one substantive predictor at a time.\n\n")
text+=(f"The condition number checks the same general problem across the whole design, rather than for one predictor. Larger values indicate combinations of columns that are harder to distinguish numerically. It is {pooled['condition_number']:.1f} in the pooled fit and {diags['components']['condition_number']:.1f} when IF and CONS replace SPEC. These values depend on scaling and coding; our design includes cohort-standardized continuous predictors, an intercept, and treatment-coded model/language indicators. We report them alongside VIF without imposing a separate condition-number cutoff.\n")
OUT.write_text('% Generated by eval/scripts/write_collinearity_explanation.py.\n'+text)
POST=OUT.with_name('collinearity_sensitivity_interpretation.tex')
text=(
    'The single-predictor fits ask how the estimate changes when the other substantive predictor is omitted. '
    'We fit distance without SPEC, and SPEC without distance, retaining model and attack-language indicators in both cases. '
    +f"These pooled comparisons use the same {pooled['n']:,} scored responses and the same standardization. The following comparison therefore reflects a change in adjustment, rather than a change in sample:\n\n"
)
text+=r'''\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
Predictor & Distance and SPEC together & Without the other predictor \\
\midrule
'''
for predictor,fit,label in [('distance','pooled_single_distance','Distance'),('spec_score','pooled_single_spec_score','SPEC')]:
    text+=f"{label} & ${num('pooled',predictor)}$ & ${num(fit,predictor)}$ "+r'\\'+'\n'
text+=r'''\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Both coefficients keep their signs, but their sizes change: the distance estimate becomes more negative without SPEC, while the SPEC estimate becomes more positive without distance. The estimated size of an association therefore depends on what else the regression accounts for. These coefficients are on the logit of expected score; they are not changes of that size in the original score. Table~\ref{tab:graded-single} reports the single-predictor intervals and the IF/CONS sensitivities.

'''
text+=(f"Omitting distance does not remove all overlap: SPEC's VIF is still {v_single:.2f} because it remains associated with the retained model/language indicators. Likewise, the decomposition has IF VIF {ifv:.2f} and CONS VIF {consv:.2f}. The simpler fits reveal sensitivity to adjustment; they do not isolate a causal effect or resolve omitted-variable bias.\n\n")
text+=r'''For H\textsubscript{3}, the implication is that ranking distance and SPEC as independent explanations requires caution. Standardization makes their coefficient units comparable, but the estimates still depend on overlapping predictors and cohort choices. We therefore consider their signs, sizes, intervals, and stability together. A larger absolute coefficient or smaller $p$-value alone does not establish a stronger and more stable predictor.

Finally, convergence answers a different question: whether the fitting algorithm reached a solution. All reported fits pass convergence, finite-estimate, and positive-definite covariance checks. Those checks make the estimates numerically usable; a successfully fitted model can still have substantial collinearity.
'''
POST.write_text('% Generated by eval/scripts/write_collinearity_explanation.py.\n'+text)
RELEASE.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
for p in [OUT,POST,Path(__file__)]:shutil.copy2(p,RELEASE/p.name)
(RELEASE/'manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps({'purpose':'Explanation of existing graded diagnostics; no model refit.','sources':{str(p.relative_to(ROOT)):hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for p in [DIAGNOSTICS,EFFECTS]},'same_cohort_and_scaling_verified':True,'code_sha256':hashlib.sha256(Path(__file__).read_bytes()).hexdigest()},indent=2)+'\n')
